    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWe can use our knowledge of fraction multiplication to square fractions.\n\nContent:\nFor example, let's evaluate the following squared fraction:\n[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^2]\n\nContent:\nFirst, we write [MATH: \\left( \\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^2] with multiplication signs:\n[MATH: ((3)/(5))^{2} = (3)/(5) \u00d7 (3)/(5)_(\u23df)_([math]\\color{blue}2[/math] times)]\n\nContent:\nNow, we calculate the product of the two fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: ((3)/(5))^{2}|= (3)/(5) \u00d7 (3)/(5); = (3 \u00d7 3)/(5 \u00d7 5); = (9)/(25)]\n\nContent:\nTherefore, we conclude that\n[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^2= \\dfrac{9}{25}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Squaring a Fraction\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2] with multiplication signs:\n[MATH: ((4)/(5))^{2}|= (4)/(5) \u00d7 (4)/(5)_(\u23df)_([math]{\\color{blue}{2}}[/math] times)]\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n[MATH: ((4)/(5))^{2}|= (4)/(5) \u00d7 (4)/(5); = (4 \u00d7 4)/(5 \u00d7 5); = (16)/(25)]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2= \\dfrac{16}{25}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((1)/(5))^{2} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (2)/(125)]\nb. [MATH: (2)/(25)]\nc. [MATH: (2)/(5)]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(125)]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(25)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nExpressed as a fraction in its simplest form, [MATH: ((2)/(5))^{2} =][FREE_ENTRY 1].\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Cubing a Fraction\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpressed as a fraction in its simplest form, [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3 =]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3] with multiplication signs:\n[MATH: ((3)/(5))^{3}|= (3)/(5) \u00d7 (3)/(5) \u00d7 (3)/(5)_(\u23df)_([math]{\\color{blue}{3}}[/math] times)]\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n[MATH: ((3)/(5))^{3}|= (3)/(5) \u00d7 (3)/(5) \u00d7 (3)/(5); = (3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3)/(5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5); = (9 \u00d7 3)/(25 \u00d7 5); = (27)/(125)]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\dfrac{27}{125}}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nExpressed as a fraction in its simplest form, [MATH: ((1)/(3))^{3} =][FREE_ENTRY 1].\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((2)/(5))^{3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (8)/(25)]\nb. [MATH: (12)/(75)]\nc. [MATH: (4)/(125)]\nd. [MATH: (8)/(125)]\ne. [MATH: (4)/(25)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Raising a Fraction to the Fourth Power\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate the value of [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4] with multiplication signs:\n[MATH: 4]\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n[MATH: ((3)/(8))^{4}|= (3)/(8) \u00d7 (3)/(8) \u00d7 (3)/(8) \u00d7 (3)/(8); = (3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3)/(8 \u00d7 8 \u00d7 8 \u00d7 8); = (3 \u00d7 3^(\u23de)^(9) \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3^(\u23de)^(9))/(8 \u00d7 8_(\u23df)_(64) \u00d7 8 \u00d7 8_(\u23df)_(64)); = (9 \u00d7 9)/(64 \u00d7 64); = (81)/(4096)]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{81}{4\\,096}}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((1)/(3))^{4} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (5)/(9)]\nb. [MATH: (4)/(27)]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(27)]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(81)]\ne. [MATH: (4)/(3)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((3)/(4))^{4} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (27)/(256)]\nb. [MATH: (81)/(256)]\nc. [MATH: (27)/(512)]\nd. [MATH: (81)/(128)]\ne. [MATH: (81)/(512)]"
